<section class="phero"><img class="phero-img" src="/assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/clipboard-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/clipboard.webp 960w" sizes="100vw" width="960" height="640" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span class="sep" aria-hidden="true">›</span><span aria-current="page">Appraisal process</span></nav><p class="tag">Process guide</p><h1>The Nebraska Insurance Appraisal Process, Step by Step</h1><p class="lede">Seven stages from the first disagreement to a signed award: what happens, who does what and where claims most often go wrong.</p><div class="btn-row"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg class="ic" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg><span>Call (844) 537-1427</span></a><a class="btn line-light" href="/free-claim-review/">Free claim review</a></div><dl class="facts"><div><dt>Stages</dt><dd>7</dd></div><div><dt>Panel</dt><dd>2 appraisers + umpire</dd></div><div><dt>Binding</dt><dd>2 of 3 signatures</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<h2>Before you begin</h2>
<p>Appraisal is a process written into most property policies to resolve one question: the amount of loss. It is not a lawsuit, it is not mediation and it does not decide coverage. Used at the right time, it can turn a stalled claim into a signed award in weeks rather than years. Used too early, it can lock in a low number.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Is appraisal the right tool?</b>Appraisal fits when coverage is accepted and the dispute is about scope or price. If the carrier denied the claim, raised an exclusion, or says you missed a deadline, talk to a Nebraska attorney first.</div>
<h2>The seven stages</h2>
<ol class="steps"><li><h3>A covered loss and a disagreement on price</h3><p>Appraisal starts when the carrier accepts that a loss is covered but you cannot agree on what it costs. Get the coverage position and the carrier’s complete estimate in writing before anything else.</p></li><li><h3>A written appraisal demand</h3><p>Either side can usually demand appraisal in writing. The demand should cite the policy’s appraisal condition, identify the claim and date of loss, and name your appraiser or state that you will name one within the policy’s time frame.</p></li><li><h3>Each side names an appraiser</h3><p>You and the carrier each select a competent and impartial appraiser. Many policies give each side a set number of days to do so after the demand. Each side generally pays its own appraiser.</p></li><li><h3>The appraisers choose an umpire</h3><p>The two appraisers agree on a neutral umpire. If they cannot agree, many policies allow either side to ask a court in the county where the property sits to appoint one.</p></li><li><h3>Inspection and independent estimates</h3><p>Each appraiser inspects the property and prepares a valuation. Good appraisers measure, photograph and price the loss independently rather than editing the other side’s numbers.</p></li><li><h3>Appraisers negotiate item by item</h3><p>The two appraisers compare estimates line by line. Agreed items are set aside, and the disagreement narrows to specific quantities, prices or repair methods. Many files settle here.</p></li><li><h3>Umpire review and a signed award</h3><p>Any items still open go to the umpire, who reviews both positions and may inspect. An award signed by any two of the three sets the amount of loss. Payment then follows under the policy’s terms, less the deductible and any prior payments.</p></li></ol>
<h2>What the award does and does not do</h2>
<ul>
<li>It sets the amount of loss for the items submitted.</li>
<li>It does not decide coverage, exclusions or policy conditions.</li>
<li>It does not award attorney fees, interest or bad-faith damages.</li>
<li>It is binding on the amount of loss, subject to limited challenges such as fraud, bias or the panel going beyond its authority.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Nebraska context</h2>
<p>Insurers in the state are regulated by the <strong>Nebraska Department of Insurance</strong> in Lincoln, which accepts consumer complaints and enforces the state’s unfair claims settlement practices law. Nebraska also has a statute that can allow a policyholder who wins a lawsuit on a policy to recover attorney fees, and courts recognize a bad-faith claim in some circumstances. Those are legal questions; an appraiser cannot advise on them. If you think your claim involves bad faith, speak with a Nebraska attorney.</p>
<p>Nebraska has no coastal wind pool, and as of this writing it is not among the states that operate a FAIR plan. If your coverage has been nonrenewed, the Department of Insurance can explain the market options available.</p>
<h2>Costs</h2>
<p>Each side typically pays its own appraiser and splits the umpire’s fee. Appraiser fees may be flat, hourly or a percentage of the increase. Get the arrangement in writing before work starts, and run the <a href="/claim-tools-nebraska/award-gap-fee-estimator/">award gap and fee estimator</a> to see whether your dispute justifies the expense.</p>
<h2>Timeline</h2>
<p>A straightforward residential hail claim can move from demand to award in six to ten weeks once both appraisers are named. Commercial, farm and contents-heavy files usually take longer, especially if an umpire hearing is needed.</p>
</article>
